0..79 Comment "//! Two migrations to one world, a value held at the seat and read there again."
80..83 Comment "//!"
84..162 Comment "//! It witnesses a capture crossing in: the second migration\'s body reads what"
163..194 Comment "//! the first held at the seat."
195..198 Comment "//!"
199..276 Comment "//! The first migration holds a value at the seat and brings its handle back."
277..357 Comment "//! `let n at seat` opens the handle, binding what it holds at the seat, and the"
358..438 Comment "//! second migration reads it there. In one process a handle is its content, and"
439..495 Comment "//! each crossing, in or back, is a commit then a deref."
496..499 Comment "//!"
500..566 Comment "//! - cites: [Vii-2007-typesafe_distributed_programming_ml5], § 3"
567..579 Comment "//! - cites:"
580..680 Comment "//!   [Rainey-2026-localmem_typedirected_adaptive_serialization_location_contentaddressable_memory],"
681..691 Comment "//!   § 5"
692..756 Comment "//! - see: `open.gandr` — what crosses back from one migration"
757..760 Comment "//!"
761..928 Comment "//! [Vii-2007-typesafe_distributed_programming_ml5]: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-78663-4_9 \"Murphy, Crary and Harper, Type-Safe Distributed Programming with ML5\""
929..1168 Comment "//! [Rainey-2026-localmem_typedirected_adaptive_serialization_location_contentaddressable_memory]: https://doi.org/10.1145/3828688 \"Rainey et al., LoCalMem: Type-Directed Adaptive Serialization for Location- and Content-Addressable Memory\""
